{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"source _meta/agents/Kourovka/paths.env\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '=== PDF page 177 ==='\npdftotext -f 177 -l 177 -layout \\\"\"'$KOUROVKA_PDF\" -\nprintf '\"'%s\\\\n' '=== PDF page 148 ==='\npdftotext -f 148 -l 148 -layout \\\"\"'$KOUROVKA_PDF\" -\nprintf '\"'%s\\\\n' '=== 20.21 synthesis ==='\nrg -l '20\\\\.21' 'Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka' | head -20 | while read -r f; do printf '\\\\n--- %s ---\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n -C 5 '\"'20\\\\.21|index 12|C_\\\\{?12|A_4' \\\"\"'$f\" | head -100; done'","aggregated_output":"=== PDF page 177 ===\n                           New Problems (21st issue, 2026)                                 177\n\n21.86. (M. Gromov, B. Weiss). A group G is said to be sofic if for every finite set\nF ⊆ G containing 1 and every ε > 0 there exist n ∈ N and a map φ : F → Sn such\nthat φ(1) = 1, d(φ(gh), φ(g)φ(h)) < ε for all g, h such that gh ∈ F , φ(g) does not\nhave fixed points for every g ∈ F \\ {1}.\n   Is every group sofic?                                               A. Lubotzky\n\n\n21.87. Assume that a finite group G has a family of d-generator subgroups whose\nindices have no common divisor. Is it true that G can be generated by d + 1 elements?\n   The answer is positive if G is solvable (L. G. Kovács, H.-S. Sim, Indag. Math., 2\n(1991), 229–232). For an arbitrary finite group G it is proved that G can be generated\nby d + 2 elements (A. Lucchini, Commun. Algebra, 28, no. 4 (2000), 1875–1880).\n                                                                                  A. Lucchini\n\n\n21.88. Is there a finite non-abelian group G of odd order, with k(G) conjugacy\nclasses, such that k(G)/|G| = 1/17?                                D. MacHale\n\n\n21.89. For n > 39, is it true that the number of conjugacy classes in the symmetric\ngroup Sn of degree n is never a divisor of the order of Sn ? In other words, is it true\nthat, for n > 39, the number p(n) of integer partitions of n is never a divisor of n!?\n                                                                                  D. MacHale\n\n\n21.90. Let Γ be a graph of diameter d. For i ∈ {1, 2, . . . , d}, let Γi be the graph on\nthe same vertex set as Γ with vertices u, w adjacent in Γi if and only if dΓ (u, w) = i.\nDoes there exist a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph Γ of diameter 3 such that Γ2\nand Γ3 are strongly regular?                                              A. A. Makhnëv\n\n\n21.91. (W. Willems). Conjecture: The sum of squares of the degrees of the irreducible\np-Brauer characters of a finite group G is at least the p′ -part of |G|.\n    This is known to be true for p = 2 (G. Malle, Adv. Math., 380 (2021), Paper\nno. 107609, 15 pp.).                                                         G. Malle\n\n\n21.92. Conjecture: The number of irreducible p-Brauer characters of a finite group\nG is bounded above by the maximum of the number of conjugacy classes k(H) in\np′ -subgroups H of G.                           G. Malle, G. Navarro, G. Robinson\n\n\n21.93. (M. Herzog, J. Schönheim). Let G be a group and let k ⩾ 2. Let H1 , . . . , Hk\nbe subgroups of G, and g1 , . . . , gk elements of G such that the cosets g1 H1 , . . . , gk Hk\nform a partition of G. Is it true that |G : Hi | = |G : Hj | for some i ̸= j?\n   This is known to be true for groups with a Sylow tower (M. A. Berger, A. Felzen-\nbaum, A. Fraenkel, Fund. Math., 128, no. 3 (1987), 139–144). Also cf. 20.99.\n                                                                                  L. Margolis\n\f=== PDF page 148 ===\n148                                   20th Issue (2022)\n\n20.21. (G. Verret). Does there exist a finite group G with two normal subgroups\nK and L, each with index 12 in G, such that K is isomorphic to L, but G/K is\nisomorphic to C12 , while G/L is isomorphic to A4 ?                   M. Conder\n\n∗ 20.22. Let G be a non-free and non-cyclic one-relator group in which every subgroup\nof infinite index is free. Is G a surface group?   B. Fine, G. Rosenberger, L. Wienke\n  ∗ Yes, it is (H. Wilton, Preprint, 2024, https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.02121).\n\n\n∗ 20.23. Is there a constant δ with 0 < δ < 1 and an integer N such that whenever\nA, B, and C are conjugacy classes in the alternating group Alt(n) each of size at least\n|Alt(n)|δ with n ⩾ N , then ABC = Alt(n)?                      M. Garonzi, A. Maróti\n  ∗ Yes, there are (D. Dona, Preprint, 2025, https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.06012).\n\n                                √\n20.24. Does the group SL2 (Z[ 2]) admit a faithful transitive amenable action?\n   This is the same as asking if it admits any co-amenable subgroup except the finite\nindex subgroups.                                               Y. Glasner, N. Monod\n\n20.25. Let n = k 2 . Let σ ∈ Sn be the product of k disjoint cycles, of lengths\n1, 3, 5, . . . , 2k − 1. Find the limiting proportion of odd entries in the σ-column of the\ncharacter table of Sn .\n    For some background, see pages 1005–1006 in (D. Gluck, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc.,\n147 (2019), 1005–1011).                                                            D. Gluck\n\n20.26. Let ϕ be the Euler totient function. Does there exist a constant a > 0 such\nthat |Aut(G)| ⩾ ϕ(|G|)a\n    a) for every finite group G?\n    b) for every finite nilpotent group G?\n   If such a constant a exists, then a ⩽ 40\n                                          41 (J. González-Sánchez, A. Jaikin-Zapirain,\nForum Math. Sigma, 3, Article ID e7, 11 p., electronic only (2015)). Also cf. Archive\n12.77.                                         J. González-Sánchez, A. Jaikin-Zapirain\n\n∗ 20.27. Let G be a finite group, p a prime number, and let |xG | denote the maximum\n                                                                 p\npower of p that divides the class size of an element x ∈ G. Suppose that there exists\na p-element g ∈ G such that |g G |p = maxx∈G |xG |p . Is it true that G has a normal\np-complement?\n    A partial answer is in (https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.03641).          I. B. Gorshkov\n  ∗ No, not necessarily, a counterexample is given by SmallGroup(192,945) (B. Sam-\nbale, Letter of 16 February 2022).\n\n20.28. Let L be a non-abelian finite simple group, and let H(L) = M (L).L be the\nuniversal perfect central extension, where M (L) is the Schur multiplier of L. Suppose\nthat G is a finite group such that the set of class sizes of G is the same as the set of\nclass sizes of H(L). Is it true that G ≃ H(L) × A, where A is an abelian group?\n    This is proved for L = Alt5 in (J. Algebra Appl., 21, no. 11 (2022), Article ID\n2250226, 8 p.).                                                          I. B. Gorshkov\n\f=== 20.21 synthesis ===\n\n--- Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/corpus/kourovka-issue-20-2022.md ---\n154-\n155-*Issue 20 (2022) · printed p. 149 · proposed by C. Chalk*\n156-\n157-Let E(l, m, n) be a group on generators a, b with the presentation ha, b | al = 1, (ab)m = bn i, where l, m, n satisfy 1/l + 1/m + 1/n < 1. Are there any values for l, m, n such that E(l, m, n) is shortlex automatic? It is known that E(6, 2, 6) is not shortlex automatic.\n158-\n159:## 20.21\n160-\n161-*Issue 20 (2022) · printed p. 150 · proposed by M. Conder*\n162-\n163:(G. Verret). Does there exist a finite group G with two normal subgroups K and L, each with index 12 in G, such that K is isomorphic to L, but G/K is isomorphic to C12 , while G/L is isomorphic to A4 ?\n164-\n165-## 20.22\n166-\n167-*Issue 20 (2022) · printed p. 150 · proposed by B. Fine, G. Rosenberger, L. Wienke*\n168-\n\n--- Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/corpus/kourovka-20-corpus.jsonl ---\n1106-{\"id\": \"20.16\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 149, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"By Hartley’s theorem (based on CFSG), if a simple locally finite group does not contain a particular finite group as a section, then it is isomorphic to a group of Lie type over a locally finite field. a) Is the same true for a definably simple locally finite group? A group is said to be definably simple if it does not contain proper definable normal subgroups. b) The same question for a definably simple locally finite group with bounded derived lengths of its solvable subgroups.\", \"proposers\": [\"A. V. Borovik\"], \"chars\": 484}\n1107-{\"id\": \"20.17\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 149, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"The normal covering number γ(G) of a finite non-cyclic group G is the mini- mum number of proper subgroups of G such that G is the union of their conjugates. a) What are the exact values of lim inf γ(Sn )/n and lim inf γ(Sn )/n? n even n odd p It is known that lim inf γ(Sn )/n ⩾ (1 − 1 − 4/π 2 )/2 ≈ 0.114411. n even b) What are the exact values of lim inf γ(An )/n and lim inf γ(An )/n? n even n odd p It is known that lim inf γ(An )/n ⩾ (1 − 1 − 4/π 2 )/2 ≈ 0.114411. n odd c) What is the exact value of lim sup γ(Sn )/n? n even It is known that lim sup γ(Sn )/n = 1/2. n odd d) What are the exact values of lim sup γ(An )/n and lim sup γ(An )/n? n odd n even\", \"proposers\": [\"D. Bubboloni\", \"C. E. Praeger\", \"P. Spiga\"], \"chars\": 662}\n1108-{\"id\": \"20.18\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 149, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"Let Rpk be the variety of class 2 nilpotent groups of exponent pk , where p is a prime number and k ⩾ 2. It is true that for every p and k there are infinitely many subquasivarieties of Rpk each of which is generated by a finite group with derived subgroup of exponent pk and does not have an independent basis of quasi-identities?\", \"proposers\": [\"A. I. Budkin\"], \"chars\": 331}\n1109-{\"id\": \"20.19\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 149, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"A subgroup H of a group G is called commensurated if for all g ∈ G, the index |H : H ∩ gHg −1 | is finite. Can a non-abelian free group (or a non-elementary hyperbolic group) contain two infinite commensurated subgroups A, B with a trivial intersection? The answer is negative if A or B is normal. P.-E. Caprace\", \"proposers\": [], \"chars\": 311}\n1110-{\"id\": \"20.20\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 149, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"Let E(l, m, n) be a group on generators a, b with the presentation ha, b | al = 1, (ab)m = bn i, where l, m, n satisfy 1/l + 1/m + 1/n < 1. Are there any values for l, m, n such that E(l, m, n) is shortlex automatic? It is known that E(6, 2, 6) is not shortlex automatic.\", \"proposers\": [\"C. Chalk\"], \"chars\": 271}\n1111:{\"id\": \"20.21\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 150, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"(G. Verret). Does there exist a finite group G with two normal subgroups K and L, each with index 12 in G, such that K is isomorphic to L, but G/K is isomorphic to C12 , while G/L is isomorphic to A4 ?\", \"proposers\": [\"M. Conder\"], \"chars\": 201}\n1112-{\"id\": \"20.22\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 150, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"Let G be a non-free and non-cyclic one-relator group in which every subgroup of infinite index is free. Is G a surface group?\", \"proposers\": [\"B. Fine\", \"G. Rosenberger\", \"L. Wienke\"], \"chars\": 125}\n1113-{\"id\": \"20.23\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 150, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"Is there a constant δ with 0 < δ < 1 and an integer N such that whenever A, B, and C are conjugacy classes in the alternating group Alt(n) each of size at least |Alt(n)|δ with n ⩾ N , then ABC = Alt(n)? M. Garonzi, A. Maroti √\", \"proposers\": [], \"chars\": 226}\n1114-{\"id\": \"20.24\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 150, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"Does the group SL2 (Z[ 2]) admit a faithful transitive amenable action? This is the same as asking if it admits any co-amenable subgroup except the finite index subgroups.\", \"proposers\": [\"Y. Glasner\", \"N. Monod\"], \"chars\": 171}\n1115-{\"id\": \"20.25\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 150, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"Let n = k 2 . Let σ ∈ Sn be the product of k disjoint cycles, of lengths 1, 3, 5, . . . , 2k − 1. Find the limiting proportion of odd entries in the σ-column of the character table of Sn . For some background, see pages 1005–1006 in (D. Gluck, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc., 147 (2019), 1005–1011).\", \"proposers\": [\"D. Gluck\"], \"chars\": 291}\n1116-{\"id\": \"20.26\", \"issue\": 20, \"year\": 2022, \"page\": 150, \"answered\": false, \"has_editor_comment\": false, \"has_later_comment\": false, \"statement\": \"Let φ be the Euler totient function. Does there exist a constant a > 0 such that |Aut(G)| ⩾ φ(|G|)a a) for every finite group G? b) for every finite nilpotent group G? 40 If such a constant a exists, then a ⩽ 41 (J. González-Sánchez, A. Jaikin-Zapirain, Forum Math. Sigma, 3, Article ID e7, 11 p., electronic only (2015)). Also cf. 12.77. J. González-Sánchez,\", \"proposers\": [\"A. Jaikin-Zapirain\"], \"chars\": 363}\n\n--- Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/20.21-two-index-twelve-normal-subgroups.md ---\n1----\n2:title: \"Kourovka Problem 20.21 — Two Index Twelve Normal Subgroups\"\n3-authors:\n4-  - \"M. Conder\"\n5-year: 2022\n6-venue: \"Kourovka Notebook No.